<p>Female pattern hair loss is a gradual thinning over the top of the scalp, most visible as a widening centre part, with the frontal hairline usually preserved. It is common, progressive and treatable. It also shares its appearance with several other causes of hair loss in women, which is why a proper diagnosis, including blood tests and a scalp examination, has to come before any discussion of treatment, let alone surgery.</p>

<p>Many women arrive at a clinic already told, by a salon, a website or a product quiz, that they have "hormonal hair fall" or that they need a transplant. Neither is a diagnosis. The same widening part can come from iron deficiency, a thyroid disorder, polycystic ovary syndrome, a long-running shed, or a scarring condition that surgery would make worse. The order of events matters: diagnose, correct what is correctable, treat the pattern medically, and only then consider whether grafting has any role. The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS advice on hair loss</a> makes the same point from the other direction, recommending a doctor's assessment before paying a private hair clinic.</p>

<h2 id="what-it-looks-like">What female pattern hair loss looks like</h2>

<p>The typical presentation is diffuse thinning concentrated over the crown and mid-scalp. When the hair is parted down the centre, the part looks wider towards the front than further back. Hair at the sides and back is usually fuller. The ponytail feels thinner, and more scalp shows under overhead lighting or when the hair is wet.</p>

<p>Unlike the male pattern, women rarely develop a receding hairline or complete baldness in any one area. <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/female-pattern-hair-loss" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet's overview of female pattern hair loss</a> describes it as diffuse thinning due to increased shedding, reduced hair volume, or both, and notes that a receding hairline is unusual unless androgen levels are raised. The <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/diseases/hair-loss/types/female-pattern" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">American Academy of Dermatology</a> describes a widening part as a typical sign and emphasises that the condition is progressive.</p>

<p>The underlying process is miniaturisation: follicles produce progressively finer, shorter hairs over successive growth cycles. Genetics plays a large part. The role of androgens is less straightforward than in men, because most women with this pattern have normal hormone levels when tested.</p>

<h2 id="conditions-that-look-like-it">The conditions that look like it</h2>

<p>This is the central reason the workup comes first. Several conditions produce thinning that looks similar at a glance and needs completely different management.</p>

<h3>Telogen effluvium</h3>

<p>A diffuse shed triggered by illness, childbirth, surgery, rapid weight loss, severe stress or a change of medication, usually appearing a few months after the event. It generally settles. A chronic form can persist for years and is easily mistaken for pattern loss. <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">Telogen effluvium versus pattern hair loss</a> explains how the two are separated.</p>

<h3>Iron deficiency and thyroid disease</h3>

<p>Both are common in women, both cause diffuse thinning, and both are correctable. Heavy periods, recent pregnancy and a diet low in iron all raise the likelihood of depleted iron stores. Thyroid disorders, whether underactive or overactive, affect hair alongside energy, weight, periods and tolerance of heat or cold. Where either is contributing, correcting it can make a real difference to the shedding.</p>

<h3>Polycystic ovary syndrome and other androgen excess</h3>

<p>Thinning that comes with acne, irregular periods or increased facial and body hair suggests excess androgens, and PCOS is the most common cause. DermNet lists these features as markers worth investigating. Here the hair is one part of a hormonal condition that deserves management in its own right, sometimes alongside a gynaecologist or endocrinologist.</p>

<h3>Postpartum shedding</h3>

<p>Heavy shedding a few months after delivery is common and usually temporary. It can, however, unmask pattern thinning that was already developing, and it frequently coexists with low iron.</p>

<h3>Traction and styling damage</h3>

<p>Tight buns, braids, extensions and heavy hairpieces pulling at the same points, repeated over years, cause traction alopecia, typically at the temples and along the hairline. Heat, bleaching and chemical straightening cause breakage that is easily mistaken for loss. Early traction alopecia can recover once the tension stops; long-standing traction can become permanent. Hard borewell water, a frequent complaint in Gurgaon, can leave hair rough and more prone to breakage, but it does not cause pattern thinning; <a href="/blog/hard-water-pollution-and-hair-fall-in-gurgaon">what hard water and pollution actually do to hair</a> separates the two.</p>

<h3>Alopecia areata</h3>

<p>Usually patchy, but a diffuse form exists that can resemble other kinds of thinning. It is an immune condition with its own treatment, covered under <a href="/alopecia-areata-treatment-in-gurgaon">alopecia areata treatment</a>.</p>

<h3>Scarring alopecias</h3>

<p>Conditions such as frontal fibrosing alopecia and lichen planopilaris destroy follicles permanently. They may present with a receding frontal hairline, thinning eyebrows, redness or scale around follicles, itching or tenderness. They are less common, but they are the most important not to miss, because hair lost to them does not return, and surgery into an active scarring process is generally avoided because grafts are unlikely to survive in inflamed tissue.</p>

<h2 id="what-a-proper-workup-involves">What a proper workup involves</h2>

<p>A thorough assessment of hair loss in women is broader than it usually is for men, and it is not a formality.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>A detailed history.</strong> When the thinning began and whether it was sudden or gradual; the menstrual pattern; pregnancies, delivery dates and breastfeeding; contraception or hormone therapy; diet and weight changes; illnesses and operations; every medicine and supplement; styling habits; and family history.</li>
  <li><strong>Examination.</strong> The part width, the hairline, the eyebrows, the sides and back, signs of androgen excess such as acne or facial hair, and any redness, scale or scarring on the scalp.</li>
  <li><strong>Trichoscopy.</strong> Magnification shows variation in hair diameter, density in different zones, and signs of inflammation or scarring. It also provides a baseline for measuring change later.</li>
  <li><strong>Blood tests where indicated.</strong> Commonly a full blood count, ferritin and thyroid function, often with vitamin D and B12, and hormone tests where the history suggests androgen excess. DermNet notes that sex hormone levels and thyroid function form part of the diagnostic workup. <a href="/blog/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">Which blood tests matter for hair fall</a> explains what each result can and cannot show.</li>
  <li><strong>Occasionally, a biopsy.</strong> If a scarring alopecia is suspected, or the diagnosis remains unclear, a small scalp biopsy settles it.</li>
</ol>

<p>At the end of that process, the diagnosis might be female pattern hair loss alone, pattern loss with a contributing factor such as low iron, or something else entirely. Each leads somewhere different.</p>

<h2 id="medical-treatment-options">Medical treatment options</h2>

<p>Female pattern hair loss is managed rather than cured. The aim is to slow progression, stabilise the hair and, in some women, improve density. Treatment is ongoing: the AAD notes that when treatment stops, the hair gained is gradually lost again within months.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Topical minoxidil</strong> is the most established first-line treatment. It takes months to show an effect, can cause a temporary increase in shedding early on, and in some women causes scalp irritation or unwanted facial hair.</li>
  <li><strong>Oral treatments prescribed off-label</strong> are used by dermatologists in selected cases. These include low-dose oral minoxidil and anti-androgen medicines such as spironolactone, particularly where there are features of androgen excess. They need a medical assessment, monitoring and a clear conversation about pregnancy, because anti-androgens must not be taken by a woman who is or may become pregnant.</li>
  <li><strong>Finasteride</strong> is not indicated for female pattern hair loss, although it is occasionally used off-label after menopause. It must not be used by women who are or may become pregnant. <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">Minoxidil and finasteride in India</a> covers the prescription rules and side effects in full.</li>
  <li><strong>Correcting contributing factors</strong> such as iron deficiency or thyroid disease, which on its own can improve shedding considerably.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjuncts</strong> such as PRP, whose evidence is modest and which works as maintenance rather than a cure. <a href="/blog/does-prp-work-for-hair-loss">Whether PRP works for hair loss</a> sets out what the published reviews support.</li>
</ul>

<p>The AAD also notes that treatments generally take six to twelve months to show whether they are working. Judging a treatment at six weeks, or switching products every month, makes it impossible to know what helped.</p>

