<h2 id="where-surgery-fits">Where a hair transplant fits, and who is not a candidate</h2>

<p>A transplant moves follicles from the back of the scalp to thinner areas. In men with pattern loss, the back of the scalp is usually resistant to thinning, which is what makes it a useful donor area. In many women, the thinning is diffuse and extends into that region too, so the hair taken may itself be miniaturising, and the donor supply may be both limited and unreliable.</p>

<p>That is why many women with female pattern hair loss are not good transplant candidates, and why an honest assessment says so. Surgery tends to be considered only when:</p>

<ul>
  <li>The diagnosis is confirmed and a scarring alopecia has been ruled out.</li>
  <li>The loss has been stabilised on medical treatment over a sustained period.</li>
  <li>The donor area has adequate density on trichoscopy and is not itself thinning.</li>
  <li>The area to be treated is defined, such as the front of the part line, rather than the whole scalp.</li>
  <li>Expectations about density are realistic, and medical treatment will continue afterwards.</li>
</ul>

<p>Women who are generally <strong>not</strong> candidates include those with active shedding, diffuse thinning throughout the scalp including the donor zone, an active or undiagnosed scarring alopecia, uncorrected medical causes such as iron deficiency or thyroid disease, or hair loss that has never been assessed. There are situations where surgery is more often suitable for women, such as traction alopecia that has become permanent, a naturally high hairline, or a stable scar, and <a href="/hair-transplant-for-women-in-gurgaon">hair transplant for women</a> describes those. The wider list of reasons to decline is in <a href="/blog/who-should-not-have-a-hair-transplant">who should not have a hair transplant</a>.</p>

<p>A transplant also does not stop female pattern hair loss progressing in the hair around the grafts. <a href="/blog/is-a-hair-transplant-permanent">Whether a hair transplant is permanent</a> explains why the native hair stays on its own trajectory.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-bring-to-the-assessment">What to bring to the assessment</h2>

<p>Much of the diagnosis rests on history, and history is easier to give accurately with a little preparation. It is worth bringing:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A timeline.</strong> When you first noticed thinning or shedding, and anything that happened in the months before it: an illness, an operation, a delivery, a new medicine, a change in diet or weight, a stressful period.</li>
  <li><strong>Your menstrual and hormonal history,</strong> including how regular your periods are, any contraception or hormone therapy, and changes around pregnancy or menopause.</li>
  <li><strong>Every medicine and supplement,</strong> with doses. Mention biotin specifically, because it can distort some blood tests, including thyroid tests.</li>
  <li><strong>Any recent blood reports,</strong> even if they were done for another reason.</li>
  <li><strong>Older photographs</strong> showing your hair a few years ago, which help establish how quickly things have changed.</li>
  <li><strong>Your styling routine,</strong> including tight styles, extensions, heat, colouring, and keratin or chemical straightening treatments.</li>
  <li><strong>Family history</strong> of thinning on both sides of the family.</li>
  <li><strong>Pregnancy plans</strong> over the next year or two, because they shape which treatments are appropriate at all.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of this needs to be exact. A rough timeline is far more useful than none, and it often points towards the diagnosis before the examination has even begun.</p>

<h2 id="what-realistic-progress-looks-like">What realistic progress looks like</h2>

<p>Honest expectations protect against both disappointment and wasted money.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Stabilisation is a result.</strong> For many women, the main achievement of treatment is that thinning stops getting worse. That is easy to overlook without photographs.</li>
  <li><strong>Improvement is gradual.</strong> Any increase in density builds over many months and shows most clearly in standardised photographs, not in the daily mirror.</li>
  <li><strong>Contributing causes change the outlook.</strong> Where iron deficiency or a thyroid disorder was driving part of the shedding, correcting it can change the picture considerably.</li>
  <li><strong>Treatment is long term.</strong> Stopping generally means the benefit fades, so choosing a treatment you can realistically keep up matters more than choosing the most intensive one.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you are noticing a widening part, the most useful next step is not a product or a procedure. It is a diagnosis. <a href="/womens-hair-loss-treatment-in-gurgaon">Women's hair loss treatment at our Gurgaon clinic</a> starts with the workup described above, as part of <a href="/hair-fall-treatment-in-gurgaon">a medical, cause-first approach to hair fall</a>, and a consultation with <a href="/dr-nyra">a dermatologist</a> is where the examination and the plan happen. Consultations are available at both our clinics, in Sector 39, Gurugram and in Rohini, Delhi.</p>

